39543372|t|Metabolic rewiring controlled by HIF-1alpha tunes IgA-producing B-cell differentiation and intestinal inflammation.
39543372|a|Germinal centers where B cells undergo clonal expansion and antibody affinity maturation are hypoxic microenvironments. However, the function of hypoxia-inducible factor (HIF)-1alpha in immunoglobulin production remains incompletely characterized. Here, we demonstrated that B cells lacking HIF-1alpha exhibited significantly lower glycolytic metabolism and impaired IgA production. Loss of HIF-1alpha in B cells affects IgA-producing B-cell differentiation and exacerbates dextran sodium sulfate (DSS)-induced colitis. Conversely, promoting HIF-1alpha stabilization via a PHD inhibitor roxadustat enhances IgA class switching and alleviates intestinal inflammation. Mechanistically, HIF-1alpha facilitates IgA class switching through acetyl-coenzyme A (acetyl-CoA) accumulation, which is essential for histone H3K27 acetylation at the Salpha region. Consequently, supplementation with acetyl-CoA improved defective IgA production in Hif1a-deficient B cells and limited experimental colitis. Collectively, these findings highlight the critical importance of HIF-1alpha in IgA class switching and the potential for targeting the HIF-1alpha-dependent metabolic-epigenetic axis to treat inflammatory bowel diseases and other inflammatory disorders.
